"""You can see the description of the task using the code specified in the title on leetcode.
This program has O(n ** 2) complexity by time and O(n ** 2) by space.

I initially set out to solve this by simulating every possible scenario, 
but it was only after writing the solution that I fully grasped the phrase "both players play optimally" which rules out that approach. 
I need to prove that the first player wins when each player makes the best possible move on their turn; 
this smells like a dynamic programming problem.

The task turned out to be more difficult than I thought, and I couldn't handle it.
We use a function that calculates the difference between the first and second players, 
assuming each makes the optimal move; 
specifically, if we pick the leftmost element, we subtract the best outcome for the remaining range from the current difference
since that represents the opponent's turn. 
The problem statement points toward this solution, but it was not at all obvious to me.
"""

# https://leetcode.com/problems/predict-the-winner/solutions/8433447/solution-by-la_castille-ia0j/?envType=daily-question&envId=2026-08-01
class Solution:
    def predictTheWinner(self, nums: List[int]) -> bool:

        @lru_cache
        def maxDiff(left: int, right: int) -> int:
            if left == right:
                return nums[left]
            return max(nums[left] - maxDiff(left + 1, right),
                       nums[right] - maxDiff(left, right - 1))

        return maxDiff(0, len(nums) - 1) >= 0
